back in 1971 just at the beginning of  when structures of proteins were being  determined it was quite a task to be  able to distribute those data if you  wanted to the protein databank  began as a grassroots effort on the part  of many scientists to figure out a way  to archive protein structures in 1998  the management of the protein databank  turned to the research collaboratory for  structural bioinformatics rcsb and we  began to manage those data the PDB had  its origins with just seven x-ray  structures of proteins it's now nearly a  hundred thousand entries in the archive  more than 40 years later there's  literally a million downloads or entries  in the archives on a daily basis the  information can be used by anyone for  any purpose without any restrictions our  CSV PDB provides the users the ability  to visualize molecules to do all kinds  of analysis and comparison of these  structures for the experts this provides  an opportunity to look at their protein  of interest and compare it to other  relatives so the PDB is a great resource  I mean it's the world's only single  resource for protein structures and it's  a wonderful resource because the whole  community uses it my lab focuses on RNA  viruses we submit them to the PDB and  then it's available to the community so  that other crystallographers can see how  we've modeled things in 2003 we formed a  consortium called the worldwide PDB in  which we brought in the people from  Europe and Asia so that we would all  work together to keep a single archive  ww PDB is a consortium of four data  centers in insurance that we have  uniform archive and that the annotation  practices are uniform as well for  several years we've been developing a  new  come on the position annotation tool  that would be uniform for all the  centers but these four centers actually  compete on how to represent the data on  the data out step the RCS P we have  decided Rutgers and we have a site at  the University of California in San  Diego  at Rutgers our primary responsibility is  to curate the data so we have people  annotators who review the data that  comes in and then they communicate with  the depositors of the data to be sure  that everything is as well represented  as possible the declaration is a fairly  complicated task it could be data  standardization like we standardized  atomic nomenclature data validation we  validate model against experimental data  and importantly we cross-reference the  data to other databases such as uniprot  or drug drug Bank this is all done to  have high quality highly standardized  validated data that could be used by  scientists and non-scientists worldwide  molecules to some people seem to be very  abstract and in our view they're very  concrete you can hold a model of a  molecule in your hand and so one very  important aspect of what we do is that  we work with students and educators in  order to have them understand molecules  teaching resources that they have  they're really really fantastic the  information that's available is for  teaching it's wonderful  they have little models and really  really helpful especially at the  undergraduate level where you're trying  to get students interested in  understanding structural biology one of  the most popular resources of the rcsb  PDB is the molecule of the month this is  an article that is written by David  Goodsell so that anybody high school  students and anybody can read it and  understand what this molecule looks like  how it interacts and does whatever it is  supposed to do we also have various  educational resources and tutorials and  lesson plans for teachers and students  protein databank has gathered up all  these educational resources into a  separate section which we call the pdb  101 which presents a less complicated  view of the protein databank so that  they're not overwhelmed by the large  amount of data and complexity that is  archived about three-quarters of our  users are not the individuals who are  depositing the data into the archive  they're people who are simply using that  information for either teaching or or  research purposes now our focus while  remaining true to our depositors is  shifting toward  the user base - information about the  protein databank available to the  students as they begin to explore  biology one of the goals that we are  striving towards is to present a  structural view of biology we want the  users to come to the PDB and understand  a biological process a disease process a  treatment program or any other aspect of  biology from a molecular structural  perspective the great wish I think would  be to be able to predict protein  structure by simply looking at the  sequence that's how this all began many  many years ago that's very important  intellectually as well as medical
 

